WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.680
SPEAKER 1: Let's shift a little bit to the question of buying sperm.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.100
So tell us a little bit about the prices paid by people who buy sperm.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.851
I presume there's some markup between what the sperm donor is paid

00:00:15.851 --> 00:00:17.475
and what they receive on the other end?

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:18.670
SPEAKER 2: Absolutely.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.650
These are businesses after all, for profit businesses.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.370
The sperm donor's walking in, being paid $100 to $150 for that sample.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:30.480
The sperm bank takes that sample and can create anywhere from zero vials--

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.440
if the sperm count isn't high enough-- to one or two,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.060
to as many as eight or nine vials from that single sample

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.900
that they've paid about $125 for.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.640
Then the sperm bank will turn around.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.820
They have to freeze it for six months.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.120
Retest the donor for HIV.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.990
And then they put that sample on the market.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.530
So there is a wide infrastructure that's in place for making sure

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.544
that that sperm is then safe to sell.

00:00:53.544 --> 00:00:55.710
And so the sperm banks-- they have a number of costs

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.180
that they're trying to manage.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.346
However, when they turn around and sell those vials,

00:00:59.346 --> 00:01:02.050
they're selling them for $500, $600, $700

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.870
depending on the specific characteristics of the sperm.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.620
Those are technical characteristics.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.350
For somebody who would like to purchase sperm in order to try to have a child,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.410
they rarely get pregnant on the first time.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.870
And you're buying multiple vials.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.950
And so this can very quickly add up to be quite an expensive proposition.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.200
SPEAKER 1: And one not typically covered by health insurance or anything else,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:20.340
right?

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.080
SPEAKER 2: That's correct.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.038
SPEAKER 1: And tell me a little bit about how--

00:01:23.038 --> 00:01:26.375
the way in which individual sperm donors are marketed to families who

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:28.000
are thinking about using a sperm donor.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.045
What kinds of information about the sperm donor are typically provided?

00:01:32.045 --> 00:01:34.420
SPEAKER 2: Yeah, well, this has really changed over time.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.410
So sperm donation has more than a century of history behind it.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.669
And for most of the 20th century it was very anonymous, very secretive.

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:44.710
And it was really the doctors who were in charge.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.350
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, the doctor would pick somebody actually

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.280
to be a sperm donor, right?

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.806
Or even in some cases, is alleged to have

00:01:48.806 --> 00:01:50.360
used his own sperm and stuff like that?

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:50.840
SPEAKER 2: Exactly.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.591
There have certainly been cases of that-- legal cases of that.

00:01:53.591 --> 00:01:56.590
So what would typically happen is a couple-- a heterosexual couple would

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.390
come in and say to the doctor, we've had trouble getting pregnant.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:00.760
They would be evaluated.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.980
And then the doctor would go into the next room.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.780
Pull a medical resident or a medical student off the floor.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.390
Ask them to provide a sample.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:09.820
It was then fresh insemination.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.050
So with the advent of AIDS in the 1980s, this became no longer tenable.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.350
And sperm banks started these large businesses

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:21.100
with the freezing and the shipping and the evaluations and the screening

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:21.850
and all of that.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.880
So over time, the amount of information about the donor

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.410
and also the consumer's agency in this process has really increased.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:34.300
So now if you go to a sperm bank, you'll get 25 to 50 pages of information

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.190
about that donor-- the health history.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.620
And answers to all kinds of questions, like-- what

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.050
was your favorite book as a child?

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.560
Why do you want to be a donor?

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.050
You know describe your hair and your skin

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.560
and your height and your manual dexterity, right?

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.200
So all of these questions are now made available to consumers,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.050
who can click through and decide what kind of characteristics

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.320
that they want in their donor.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.170
SPEAKER 1: Are there particular traits that are particularly valued?

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:00.827
You're paid more for by the sperm bank, or particularly valued by consumers?

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:03.410
SPEAKER 2: Yeah, and so this is another interesting difference

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.290
that emerges when you're looking at sperm banks versus egg agencies.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:09.070
And so sperm banks have a number of requirements

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.590
that really reflect our cultural expectations for masculinity.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.710
So sperm donors have to be at least five feet eight or five

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.400
feet nine inches tall.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.150
Short sperm doesn't sell.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:23.020
Men have to be enrolled in college or have a four year degree.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.020
And if you then switch over to the egg agencies,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.895
they don't have that same height requirement.

00:03:26.895 --> 00:03:28.960
They don't have that same education requirement.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.170
So we know that your height and your education

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.610
does not appear in any one place on your DNA,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:37.470
but that you inherit elements of those characteristics from both parents.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.590
And so why do we need tall, educated sperm donors,

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.426
but we don't need tall, educated egg donors?

00:03:42.426 --> 00:03:45.300
And I think that's one of the places where when you compare these two

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.950
markets, you can really see cultural norms around gender

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.832
emerging and infusing the way that the market operates.

00:03:51.832 --> 00:03:53.290
SPEAKER 1: So what about ethnicity?

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.180
Are there particular ethnicities that are hard to find in the market?

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.102
Are there premiums paid for some ethnicities?

00:03:58.102 --> 00:04:01.310
SPEAKER 2: Well, this again is another difference between sperm banks and egg

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:01.860
agencies.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:06.480
So sperm banks will pay the same rate to all of the men in the bank.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.847
There are some banks that, in the past, have paid extra for men with PhDs.

00:04:10.847 --> 00:04:12.930
But in terms of ethnicity, sperm banks will really

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.329
just complain about the difficulty they have in recruiting men

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.310
from a diverse range of backgrounds.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.700
So they might bend their expectations in terms

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.200
of those height requirements or their education requirements

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.830
in order to get men from a diverse array of backgrounds in the door.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.760
In egg agencies, they actually do pay women more

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.330
if they come from racial or ethnic backgrounds

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:33.980
that they consider hard to recruit.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:37.900
So African-American women donors, Asian-American women donors

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.155
will often command a few thousand dollars more than white donors.

00:04:41.155 --> 00:04:43.280
And that's interesting sociologically, because this

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.740
is one of the few markets where women of color

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.590
do better than white women in terms of labor market outcomes.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:49.850
SPEAKER 1: That's fascinating.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.770
Can you talk a little bit about the differences between anonymous

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.630
versus open identification sperm banks?

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.090
SPEAKER 2: So again this is sort of a historical shift.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.545
In the early days of sperm donation, as I mentioned,

00:05:01.545 --> 00:05:03.170
it was completely anonymous, secretive.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.650
Often records weren't even kept.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.320
So certainly recipients had no idea who the donor was and vice versa.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.780
And this really shifted along with demographic changes

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.570
in the number of same sex couples.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.730
So there was a sperm bank that grew out of the feminist women's health

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:16.250
movement.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:20.080
And they were really oriented to serving the lesbians and single women--

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.980
in the late 70s, early 80s-- who were barred from mainstream sperm banks.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:28.220
So mainstream sperm banks would not send sperm to lesbians or single women,

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.690
saying that they didn't actually have infertility.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:34.230
So this feminist-oriented sperm bank opens up.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.171
And they start selling sperm to lesbians and single women who said,

00:05:37.171 --> 00:05:39.795
it'd be really great if we could find out more about our donor,

00:05:39.795 --> 00:05:42.130
or maybe our children could meet the donor someday.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.400
So the very first-- what they call &amp;quot;identity release&amp;quot;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.580
program opened in around 1980, 1981.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.500
And that became something that then migrated to more mainstream sperm banks

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:55.080
as the mainstream sperm banks became more open to serving these same sex

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.405
couples and single women that they had previously been discriminating against.

00:05:58.405 --> 00:06:00.780
SPEAKER 1: Are there any noticeable difference these days

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.590
from the ways in which lesbian women or single women select sperm donors

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.960
as opposed to heterosexual couples?

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.230
SPEAKER 2: So the number of heterosexual couples who have to go to sperm banks

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.200
to treat their infertility has actually dramatically reduced.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.420
And that happened after the development of ICSI-- I-C-S-I,

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:18.870
which is intracytoplasmic sperm injection.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.550
In the mid-1990s, this is a technology that-- if they

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.430
could find a single sperm in the male body,

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.670
they could inject it directly into the egg.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.830
And so heterosexual couples that can afford IVF with ICSI,

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.560
now really prefer that technology because it allows them

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.490
to have a genetically-related child.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.290
So this is that shift I was talking about,

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.800
where the sperm banks were mostly serving heterosexual clients.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.150
They saw that population drop in the 90s.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.000
They start opening up their doors to the lesbians and single women.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.800
When I talk to sperm bank owners and people who work in sperm banks,

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.900
they say absolutely that the considerations

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.440
are different for heterosexual couples and lesbians.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.680
Lesbians have no reason to hide the fact that they needed to purchase sperm.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.230
Whereas heterosexual couples were really focused

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:03.860
on trying to match the husband's physiognomy in order to make sure

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.440
that people didn't guess that he wasn't the real father.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.800
So the sort of secrecy and anonymity, along with the demographics

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.650
of the customers, have really changed how this market works.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.130
SPEAKER 1: Can you tell us a little bit about the international market

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:15.629
for sperm?

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:20.456
So one thing I've read is that actually Danish sperm is particularly exported?

00:07:20.456 --> 00:07:21.080
SPEAKER 2: Yes.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.370
So this is the phenomenon of the Viking sperm banks.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:27.900
So the-- Denmark is actually the largest exporter of sperm in the world.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:32.600
They have a website that is festooned with little blond, blue-eyed babies.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:35.960
And this is the idea that they really have sperm that people want.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:39.330
And part of why Denmark has become such a large exporter of sperm

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:42.210
is because several of the other European countries

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:47.140
have exerted quite a lot of regulation on sperm banks and sperm donation.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.010
So one example here is France.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.540
It's a national sperm bank, a nationalized bank.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.990
But they only allow heterosexual couples access to sperm.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.490
So lesbians and single women in France then need to turn elsewhere.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.520
So this is part of the market that the Viking sperm bank is serving.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:02.810
SPEAKER 1: Great.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.726
And just to pick two things out of that-- one,

00:08:04.726 --> 00:08:07.170
is it seems to be that there's a bit of a eugenic impulse.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:09.890
And we'll talk about this at a later portion of the course.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:12.570
Certain traits are valued or thought to be valued.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.950
The other this idea of trying to circumvent

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:18.100
domestic regulation by importing.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:21.040
So they don't like the way in which France regulates sperm.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.960
So for a lesbian couple, they may import sperm from Denmark

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.197
rather than dealing with the French authorities and their regulation.

00:08:28.197 --> 00:08:29.280
SPEAKER 2: Right, exactly.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:30.910
SPEAKER 1: Well, the last thing I want to ask you about sperm

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.020
is-- we've talked a lot about sperm donation

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:36.570
in the sense of men who sell sperm and people who buy sperm.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:41.590
How are things different at all between true donation, altruistic donation,

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:42.490
if you will?

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.070
SPEAKER 2: Well, one of the really interesting elements

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:48.750
of this market that has been emerging in the last few years

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:51.490
is really due to internet traffic in sperm.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:54.360
So there's been a couple of journalistic exposes

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.220
of men who offer their sperm online for free to all comers.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:01.750
And so this is an example of altruistic sperm donation.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.530
I'm not sure if it's quite the type that you were asking me about.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:08.380
But that is a completely unknown underbelly of this market.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:11.240
And we are just starting sort of sociological and anthropological

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.907
studies of how that market is operating.

00:09:12.907 --> 00:09:14.656
SPEAKER 1: And later in this course, we'll

00:09:14.656 --> 00:09:17.260
actually talk about the case of the Craigslist sperm donor

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:20.480
who the state of Kansas went after for child support.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:23.540
And that will be something we'll talk about-- which is great.

